  "worked_solution": "The range of $\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5x+9}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5x+9} & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{5x+9} & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14239347/assets/8ece70a3c8855e00a0232248cb922a40c8c9756ef67ea12b012eb719c8ee3bd1.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
